cg32_t ), intent ( inout ) :: gen integer ( int64 ), intent ( in ) :: span_minus_1 !! Inclusive upper bound, read as unsigned. integer ( int64 ) :: r integer ( int64 ) :: mask if ( span_minus_1 == 0_int64 ) then r = 0_int64 return end if mask = rejection_mask ( span_minus_1 ) do r = iand ( gen % next_u64 (), mask ) if (. not . u64_less ( span_minus_1 , r )) exit end do end function pcg32_uniform_below","tags":"","url":"proc/pcg32_uniform_below.html"},{"title":"rejection_mask – PIC","text":"private pure function rejection_mask(span_minus_1) result(mask) Smallest 2**k - 1 that covers span_minus_1 read as unsigned. Smears the highest set bit down over every bit below it. A span_minus_1 of -1, which as unsigned is 2**64 - 1, gives a mask of\n-1, under which every draw is accepted – which is right, because\nthat span is the whole word. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: span_minus_1 One less than the number of distinct values wanted, unsigned. Return Value integer(kind=int64) Calls proc~~rejection_mask~2~~CallsGraph proc~rejection_mask~2 rejection_mask proc~u64_shr u64_shr proc~rejection_mask~2->proc~u64_shr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~rejection_mask~2~~CalledByGraph proc~rejection_mask~2 rejection_mask proc~pcg32_uniform_below pcg32_uniform_below proc~pcg32_uniform_below->proc~rejection_mask~2 proc~splitmix64_uniform_below splitmix64_uniform_below proc~splitmix64_uniform_below->proc~rejection_mask~2 proc~pcg32_next_bernoulli_ppm pcg32_next_bernoulli_ppm proc~pcg32_next_bernoulli_ppm->proc~pcg32_uniform_below proc~pcg32_next_range pcg32_next_range proc~pcg32_next_range->proc~pcg32_uniform_below proc~splitmix64_next_bernoulli_ppm splitmix64_next_bernoulli_ppm proc~splitmix64_next_bernoulli_ppm->proc~splitmix64_uniform_below proc~splitmix64_next_range splitmix64_next_range proc~splitmix64_next_range->proc~splitmix64_uniform_below interface~next_bernoulli_ppm next_bernoulli_ppm interface~next_bernoulli_ppm->proc~pcg32_next_bernoulli_ppm interface~next_bernoulli_ppm->proc~splitmix64_next_bernoulli_ppm interface~next_range next_range interface~next_range->proc~pcg32_next_range interface~next_range->proc~splitmix64_next_range Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: shift Source Code pure function rejection_mask ( span_minus_1 ) result ( mask ) !! Smallest `2**k - 1` that covers `span_minus_1` read as unsigned. !! !! Smears the highest set bit down over every bit below it. A !! `span_minus_1` of -1, which as unsigned is 2**64 - 1, gives a mask of !! -1, under which every draw is accepted -- which is right, because !! that span is the whole word. integer ( int64 ), intent ( in ) :: span_minus_1 !! One less than the number of distinct values wanted, unsigned. integer ( int64 ) :: mask integer ( default_int ) :: shift mask = span_minus_1 shift = 1_default_int do while ( shift < 64_default_int ) mask = ior ( mask , u64_shr ( mask , shift )) shift = shift * 2_default_int end do end function rejection_mask","tags":"","url":"proc/rejection_mask~2.html"},{"title":"scale_by_mean – PIC","text":"private pure function scale_by_mean(x_q32, mean) result(r) Multiply a Q32.32 value by an integer mean and drop the fraction. Done in two halves so that nothing overflows. The integer part of x_q32 is at most 9 ln(4096), under 75, so the first product cannot\nexceed 75 * huge(int32). The fractional part is below 2 32 and mean is at most huge(int32), so the second product is at most\n(2 32 - 1) * (2 *31 - 1); adding the rounding term below still\nleaves it under huge(int64), with about 4e9 to spare. The fraction is rounded to nearest rather than truncated. Truncating\nwould lose on average half a unit per draw, which is invisible at a\nmean of 4000 ticks and a 23% error at a mean of 2. Rounding shrinks that but does not remove it. Discretising an\nexponential to integers leaves E[round(mean*X)] at exp(-1/(2*mean)) / (1 - exp(-1/mean)) , which is 4.0% low at a mean\nof 1, 1.0% at 2, 0.46% at 3 and 0.04% at 10. Above about 100 it is\ngone. That is inherent to an integer result, not a defect in the\nscaling, but a caller working in units that small should know the\nmean it gets is not quite the mean it asked for. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: x_q32 Deviate in Q32.32 fixed point. integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: mean Mean, in [0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN] . Return Value integer(kind=int64) Calls proc~~scale_by_mean~~CallsGraph proc~scale_by_mean scale_by_mean proc~u64_shr u64_shr proc~scale_by_mean->proc~u64_shr Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~scale_by_mean~~CalledByGraph proc~scale_by_mean scale_by_mean proc~pcg32_next_exponential_int pcg32_next_exponential_int proc~pcg32_next_exponential_int->proc~scale_by_mean proc~splitmix64_next_exponential_int splitmix64_next_exponential_int proc~splitmix64_next_exponential_int->proc~scale_by_mean interface~next_exponential_int next_exponential_int interface~next_exponential_int->proc~pcg32_next_exponential_int interface~next_exponential_int->proc~splitmix64_next_exponential_int proc~pcg32_next_poisson_int pcg32_next_poisson_int proc~pcg32_next_poisson_int->proc~pcg32_next_exponential_int proc~splitmix64_next_poisson_int splitmix64_next_poisson_int proc~splitmix64_next_poisson_int->proc~splitmix64_next_exponential_int interface~next_poisson_int next_poisson_int interface~next_poisson_int->proc~pcg32_next_poisson_int interface~next_poisson_int->proc~splitmix64_next_poisson_int Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function scale_by_mean ( x_q32 , mean ) result ( r ) !! Multiply a Q32.32 value by an integer mean and drop the fraction. !! !! Done in two halves so that nothing overflows. The integer part of !! `x_q32` is at most 9*ln(4096), under 75, so the first product cannot !! exceed 75 * huge(int32). The fractional part is below 2**32 and !! `mean` is at most huge(int32), so the second product is at most !! (2**32 - 1) * (2**31 - 1); adding the rounding term below still !! leaves it under huge(int64), with about 4e9 to spare. !! !! The fraction is rounded to nearest rather than truncated. Truncating !! would lose on average half a unit per draw, which is invisible at a !! mean of 4000 ticks and a 23% error at a mean of 2. !! !! Rounding shrinks that but does not remove it. Discretising an !! exponential to integers leaves `E[round(mean*X)]` at !! `exp(-1/(2*mean)) / (1 - exp(-1/mean))`, which is 4.0% low at a mean !! of 1, 1.0% at 2, 0.46% at 3 and 0.04% at 10. Above about 100 it is !! gone. That is inherent to an integer result, not a defect in the !! scaling, but a caller working in units that small should know the !! mean it gets is not quite the mean it asked for. integer ( int64 ), intent ( in ) :: x_q32 !! Deviate in Q32.32 fixed point. integer ( int64 ), intent ( in ) :: mean !! Mean, in `[0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN]`. integer ( int64 ) :: r r = u64_shr ( x_q32 , 32_default_int ) * mean & + u64_shr ( iand ( x_q32 , Q32_MASK ) * mean + Q32_HALF , 32_default_int ) end function scale_by_mean","tags":"","url":"proc/scale_by_mean.html"},{"title":"splitmix64_next_bernoulli_ppm – PIC","text":"private  function splitmix64_next_bernoulli_ppm(gen, ppm) result(r) True with probability ppm in a million, from a splitmix64_t generator. Arg