ype Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function heap_is_empty ( self ) result ( empty ) !! `.true.` when the heap holds no entries. class ( heap_t ), intent ( in ) :: self logical :: empty empty = ( self % n_entries == 0 ) end function heap_is_empty","tags":"","url":"proc/heap_is_empty.html"},{"title":"heap_size – PIC","text":"private pure function heap_size(self) result(n) Number of entries currently in the heap. Type Bound heap_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(in) :: self Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Source Code pure function heap_size ( self ) result ( n ) !! Number of entries currently in the heap. class ( heap_t ), intent ( in ) :: self integer ( default_int ) :: n n = self % n_entries end function heap_size","tags":"","url":"proc/heap_size.html"},{"title":"precedes – PIC","text":"private pure function precedes(self, left, right) result(first) .true. when entry left must pop before entry right . Compares (key, insertion index) lexicographically, which makes the\nordering total and the tie behaviour FIFO. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(in) :: self integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: left integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: right Return Value logical Called by proc~~precedes~~CalledByGraph proc~precedes precedes proc~sift_down sift_down proc~sift_down->proc~precedes proc~sift_up sift_up proc~sift_up->proc~precedes proc~heap_build_from heap_t%heap_build_from proc~heap_build_from->proc~sift_down proc~heap_pop heap_t%heap_pop proc~heap_pop->proc~sift_down proc~heap_push heap_t%heap_push proc~heap_push->proc~sift_up proc~a_star a_star proc~a_star->proc~heap_pop proc~a_star->proc~heap_push proc~dijkstra dijkstra proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_pop proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_push Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function precedes ( self , left , right ) result ( first ) !! `.true.` when entry `left` must pop before entry `right`. !! !! Compares `(key, insertion index)` lexicographically, which makes the !! ordering total and the tie behaviour FIFO. class ( heap_t ), intent ( in ) :: self integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: left integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: right logical :: first if ( self % keys ( left ) == self % keys ( right )) then first = ( self % insertion ( left ) < self % insertion ( right )) else if ( self % max_heap ) then first = ( self % keys ( left ) > self % keys ( right )) else first = ( self % keys ( left ) < self % keys ( right )) end if end function precedes","tags":"","url":"proc/precedes.html"},{"title":"root_entry – PIC","text":"private  function root_entry(self, key, payload, err) result(ok) Fetch the root entry, reporting an error when the heap is empty. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(in) :: self integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: key integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: payload type( error_t ), intent(out), optional :: err Return Value logical Calls proc~~root_entry~~CallsGraph proc~root_entry root_entry proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~root_entry->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~root_entry~~CalledByGraph proc~root_entry root_entry proc~heap_peek heap_t%heap_peek proc~heap_peek->proc~root_entry proc~heap_pop heap_t%heap_pop proc~heap_pop->proc~root_entry proc~a_star a_star proc~a_star->proc~heap_pop proc~dijkstra dijkstra proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_pop Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code function root_entry ( self , key , payload , err ) result ( ok ) !! Fetch the root entry, reporting an error when the heap is empty. class ( heap_t ), intent ( in ) :: self integer ( int64 ), intent ( out ) :: key integer ( int32 ), intent ( out ) :: payload type ( error_t ), intent ( out ), optional :: err logical :: ok if ( self % n_entries == 0 ) then key = 0_int64 payload = 0_int32 ok = . false . if ( present ( err )) then call err % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , \"pic_heap: cannot read the root of an empty heap\" ) end if return end if key = self % keys ( 1 ) payload = self % payloads ( 1 ) ok = . true . end function root_entry","tags":"","url":"proc/root_entry.html"},{"title":"grow_int32 – PIC","text":"private  subroutine grow_int32(arr, n_keep, new_cap) integer(int32) counterpart of grow_int64 . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(inout), allocatable :: arr (:) integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: n_keep integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: new_cap Called by proc~~grow_int32~~CalledByGraph proc~grow_int32 grow_int32 proc~heap_reserve heap_t%heap_reserve proc~heap_reserve->proc~grow_int32 proc~heap_build_from heap_t%heap_build_from proc~heap_build_from->proc~heap_reserve proc~heap_init heap_t%heap_init proc~heap_init->proc~heap_reserve proc~heap_push heap_t%heap_push proc~heap_push->proc~heap_reserve proc~a_star a_star proc~a_star->proc~heap_init proc~a_star->proc~heap_push proc~dijkstra dijkstra proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_init proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_push Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=int32), private, allocatable :: tmp (:) Source Code subroutine grow_int32 ( arr , n_keep , new_cap ) !! `integer(int32)` counterpart of `grow_int64`. integer ( int32 ), allocatable , intent ( inout ) :: arr (:) integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: n_keep integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: new_cap integer ( int32 ), allocatable :: tmp (:) integer ( default_int ) :: i if (. not . allocated ( arr )) then allocate ( arr ( new_cap )) return end if allocate ( tmp ( n_keep )) do i = 1 , n_keep tmp ( i ) = arr ( i ) end do deallocate ( arr ) allocate ( arr ( new_cap )) do i = 1 , n_keep arr ( i ) = tmp ( i ) end do deallocate ( tmp ) end subroutine grow_int32","tags":"","url":"proc/grow_int32.html"},{"title":"grow_int64 – PIC","text":"private  subroutine grow_int64(arr, n_keep, new_cap) Reallocate arr to new_cap slots, keeping its first n_keep values. Deliberately written as explicit allocate/copy/deallocate rather than move_alloc or reallocation-on-assignment: both have historically\nbeen unreliable on nvfortran and LFortran, and this path runs only\nO(log n) times over the life of a heap. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int64), intent(inout), allocatable :: arr (:) integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: n_keep integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: new_cap Called by proc~~grow_int64~~CalledByGraph proc~grow_int64 grow_int64 proc~heap_reserve heap_t%heap_reserve proc~heap_reserve->proc~grow_int64 proc~heap_build_from heap_t%heap_build_from proc~heap_build_from->proc~heap_reserve proc~heap_init heap_t%heap_init proc~heap_init->proc~heap_reserve proc~heap_push heap_t%heap_push proc~heap_push->proc~heap_reserve proc~a_star a_star proc~a_star->proc~heap_init proc~a_star->proc~heap_push proc~dijkstra dijkstra proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_init proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_push Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=int64), private, allocatable :: tmp (:) Source Code subroutine grow_int64 ( arr , n_keep , new_cap ) !! Reallocate `arr` to `new_cap` slots, keeping its first `n_keep` values. !! !! Deliberately written as explicit allocate/copy/deallocate rather than !! `move_alloc` or reallocation-on-assignment: both have historically !! been unreliable on nvfortran and LFortran, and this path runs only !! O(log n) times over the life of a heap. integer ( int64 ), allocatable , intent ( inout ) :: arr (:) integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: n_keep integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: new_cap integer ( int64 ), allocatable :: tmp (:) integer ( default_int ) :: i if (. not . allocated ( arr )) then allocate ( arr ( new_cap )) return end if allocate ( tmp ( n_keep )) do i = 1 , n_keep tmp ( i ) = arr ( i ) end do deallocate ( arr ) allocate ( arr ( new_cap )) do i = 1 , n_keep arr ( i ) = tmp ( i ) end do deallocate ( tmp ) end subroutine grow_int64","tags":"","url":"proc/grow_int64.html"},{"title":"heap_build_from – PIC","text":"private  subroutine heap_build_from(self, keys, payloads, err) Replace the heap contents with keys / payloads , heapified in O(n). This is Floyd’s bottom-up heapify, not size(keys) repeated pushes.\nThe two arrays must have the same length, otherwise err reports ERROR_VALIDATION and the heap is left untouched. Equal keys pop in\nincreasing array-index order, matching what pushing the arrays in\norder would have produced. Type Bound heap_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(inout) :: self integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: keys (:) integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: payloads (:) type( error_t ), intent(out), optional :: err Calls proc~~heap_build_from~~CallsGraph proc~heap_build_from heap_t%heap_build_from proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~heap_build_from->proc~error_set proc~heap_reserve heap_t%heap_reserve proc~heap_build_from->proc~heap_reserve proc~sift_down sift_down proc~heap_build_from->proc~sift_down proc~grow_int32 grow_int32 proc~heap_reserve->proc~grow_int32 proc~grow_int64 grow_int64 proc~heap_reserve->proc~grow_int64 proc~heap_capacity heap_t%heap_capacity proc~heap_reserve->proc~heap_capacity proc~precedes precedes proc~sift_down->proc~precedes proc~swap_entries swap_entries proc~sift_down->proc~swap_entries Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=default_int), private :: n Source Code subroutine heap_build_from ( self , keys , payloads , err ) !! Replace the heap contents with `keys`/`payloads`, heapified in O(n). !! !! This is Floyd's bottom-up heapify, not `size(keys)` repeated pushes. !! The two arrays must have the same length, otherwise `err` reports !! `ERROR_VALIDATION` and the heap is left untouched. Equal keys pop in !! increasing array-index order, matching what pushing the arrays in !! order would have produced. class ( heap_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self integer ( int64 ), intent ( in ) :: keys (:) integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ) :: payloads (:) type ( error_t ), intent ( out ), optional :: err integer ( default_int ) :: n , i n = int ( size ( keys ), default_int ) if ( int ( size ( payloads ), default_int ) /= n ) then if ( present ( err )) then call err % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , \"pic_heap: build_from requires keys and payloads of equal length\" ) end if return end if call self % reserve ( n ) do i = 1 , n self % keys ( i ) = keys ( i ) self % payloads ( i ) = payloads ( i ) self % insertion ( i ) = int ( i , int64 ) end do self % n_entries = n self % n_pushed = int ( n , int64 ) do i = n / 2 , 1 , - 1 call sift_down ( self , i ) end do end subroutine heap_build_from","tags":"","url":"proc/heap_build_from.html"},{"title":"heap_clear – PIC","text":"private  subroutine heap_clear(self) Remove every entry but keep the allocated storage for reuse. The insertion counter is reset as well, so a cleared heap behaves\nexactly like a freshly initialised one with the same capacity and the\nsame min/max ordering. Type Bound heap_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(inout) :: self Source Code subroutine heap_clear ( self ) !! Remove every entry but keep the allocated storage for reuse. !! !! The insertion counter is reset as well, so a cleared heap behaves !! exactly like a freshly initialised one with the same capacity and the !! same min/max ordering. class ( heap_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self self % n_entries = 0 self % n_pushed = 0_int64 end subroutine heap_clear","tags":"","url":"proc/heap_clear.html"},{"title":"heap_destroy – PIC","text":"private  subroutine heap_destroy(self) Release the heap storage and reset it to empty. Type Bound heap_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(inout) :: self Called by proc~~heap_destroy~~CalledByGraph proc~heap_destroy heap_t%heap_destroy proc~a_star a_star proc~a_star->proc~heap_destroy proc~dijkstra dijkstra proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_destroy Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine heap_destroy ( self ) !! Release the heap storage and reset it to empty. class ( heap_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self if ( allocated ( self % keys )) deallocate ( self % keys ) if ( allocated ( self % payloads )) deallocate ( self % payloads ) if ( allocated ( self % insertion )) deallocate ( self % insertion ) self % n_entries = 0 self % n_pushed = 0_int64 end subroutine heap_destroy","tags":"","url":"proc/heap_destroy.html"},{"title":"heap_init – PIC","text":"private  subroutine heap_init(self, cap, max_heap) Initialise the heap, discarding any previous contents. cap pre-allocates storage for at least that many entries (default DEFAULT_CAPACITY ); a non-positive cap allocates nothing yet. max_heap = .true. selects max-heap ordering, the default is a\nmin-heap. Calling init on a heap that already holds entries resets\nit to empty but keeps any storage it had already acquired. Type Bound heap_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(inout) :: self integer(kind=default_int), intent(in), optional :: cap logical, intent(in), optional :: max_heap Calls proc~~heap_init~~CallsGraph proc~heap_init heap_t%heap_init proc~heap_reserve heap_t%heap_reserve proc~heap_init->proc~heap_reserve proc~grow_int32 grow_int32 proc~heap_reserve->proc~grow_int32 proc~grow_int64 grow_int64 proc~heap_reserve->proc~grow_int64 proc~heap_capacity heap_t%heap_capacity proc~heap_reserve->proc~heap_capacity Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~heap_init~~CalledByGraph proc~heap_init heap_t%heap_init proc~a_star a_star proc~a_star->proc~heap_init proc~dijkstra dijkstra proc~dijkstra->proc~heap_init Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: wanted Source Code subroutine heap_init ( self , cap , max_heap ) !! Initialise the heap, discarding any previous contents. !! !! `cap` pre-allocates storage for at least that many entries (default !! `DEFAULT_CAPACITY`); a non-positive `cap` allocates nothing yet. !! `max_heap = .true.` selects max-heap ordering, the default is a !! min-heap. Calling `init` on a heap that already holds entries resets !! it to empty but keeps any storage it had already acquired. class ( heap_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ), optional :: cap logical , intent ( in ), optional :: max_heap integer ( default_int ) :: wanted self % n_entries = 0 self % n_pushed = 0_int64 self % max_heap = . false . if ( present ( max_heap )) self % max_heap = max_heap wanted = DEFAULT_CAPACITY if ( present ( cap )) wanted = cap call self % reserve ( wanted ) end subroutine heap_init","tags":"","url":"proc/heap_init.html"},{"title":"heap_peek – PIC","text":"private  subroutine heap_peek(self, key, payload, err) Return the root entry without removing it. Behaves exactly like pop on an empty heap: key and payload are\nzeroed and err reports ERROR_VALIDATION . Type Bound heap_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(in) :: self integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: key integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: payload type( error_t ), intent(out), optional :: err Calls proc~~heap_peek~~CallsGraph proc~heap_peek heap_t%heap_peek proc~root_entry root_entry proc~heap_peek->proc~root_entry proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~root_entry->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial logical, private :: ok Source Code subroutine heap_peek ( self , key , payload , err ) !! Return the root entry without removing it. !! !! Behaves exactly like `pop` on an empty heap: `key` and `payload` are !! zeroed and `err` reports `ERROR_VALIDATION`. class ( heap_t ), intent ( in ) :: self integer ( int64 ), intent ( out ) :: key integer ( int32 ), intent ( out ) :: payload type ( error_t ), intent ( out ), optional :: err logical :: ok ok = root_entry ( self , key , payload , err ) end subroutine heap_peek","tags":"","url":"proc/heap_peek.html"},{"title":"heap_pop – PIC","text":"private  subroutine heap_pop(self, key, payload, err) Remove and return the root entry (minimum, or maximum for a max-heap). Popping an empty heap is an error, not a crash: key and payload are set to zero and err reports ERROR_VALIDATION . err is\noptional; when it is absent an empty pop simply yields zeros. Type Bound heap_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( heap_t ), intent(inout) :: self integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: key integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: payload type( error_t ), intent(out), optional :: err Calls proc~~heap_pop~~CallsGraph proc~heap_pop heap_t%heap_pop proc~root_entry root_entry proc~heap_pop->proc~root_entry proc~sift_down sift_down proc~heap_pop->proc~sift_down proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~root_entry->proc~error_set proc~precedes precedes proc~sift_down->proc~precedes proc~swap_