idiom: build a vector while parsing an input of\nunknown length, then take the result into the permanent array. as_array copies; this does not. Type Bound vector_int32_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( vector_int32_t ), intent(inout) :: self integer(kind=int32), intent(out), allocatable :: array (:) type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Calls proc~~vec_int32_take~~CallsGraph proc~vec_int32_take vector_int32_t%vec_int32_take proc~vec_int32_capacity vector_int32_t%vec_int32_capacity proc~vec_int32_take->proc~vec_int32_capacity proc~vec_int32_shrink_to_fit vector_int32_t%vec_int32_shrink_to_fit proc~vec_int32_take->proc~vec_int32_shrink_to_fit proc~vec_int32_shrink_to_fit->proc~vec_int32_capacity proc~error_raise error_raise proc~vec_int32_shrink_to_fit->proc~error_raise proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~error_raise->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure subroutine vec_int32_take ( self , array , err ) !! Hand the storage over to `array`, exactly sized, without copying the !! elements. The vector is left empty and without storage. !! !! This is the load-time idiom: build a vector while parsing an input of !! unknown length, then `take` the result into the permanent array. !! `as_array` copies; this does not. class ( vector_int32_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self integer ( int32 ), allocatable , intent ( out ) :: array (:) type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ), optional :: err ! Spare capacity would otherwise be handed over as live elements, so the ! shrink is part of the contract rather than an optimisation. call vec_int32_shrink_to_fit ( self , err ) if ( vec_int32_capacity ( self ) /= self % n_items ) return if (. not . allocated ( self % items )) allocate ( self % items ( 0 )) call move_alloc ( self % items , array ) self % n_items = 0 end subroutine vec_int32_take","tags":"","url":"proc/vec_int32_take.html"},{"title":"vec_int64_append – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine vec_int64_append(self, values, err) Append many elements, growing once rather than once per element. Type Bound vector_int64_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( vector_int64_t ), intent(inout) :: self integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: values (:) type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Calls proc~~vec_int64_append~~CallsGraph proc~vec_int64_append vector_int64_t%vec_int64_append proc~error_raise error_raise proc~vec_int64_append->proc~error_raise proc~vec_int64_capacity vector_int64_t%vec_int64_capacity proc~vec_int64_append->proc~vec_int64_capacity proc~vec_int64_grow vec_int64_grow proc~vec_int64_append->proc~vec_int64_grow proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~error_raise->proc~error_set proc~vec_int64_grow->proc~error_raise proc~vec_int64_grow->proc~vec_int64_capacity Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: n_new Source Code pure subroutine vec_int64_append ( self , values , err ) !! Append many elements, growing once rather than once per element. class ( vector_int64_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self integer ( int64 ), intent ( in ) :: values (:) type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ), optional :: err integer ( default_int ) :: n_new n_new = size ( values , kind = default_int ) if ( n_new == 0 ) return ! Rearranged from `n_items + n_new > MAX` so that the sum is never ! formed; the subtraction cannot overflow because `n_items` is ! non-negative and PIC_VECTOR_MAX_CAPACITY is `huge`. if ( n_new > PIC_VECTOR_MAX_CAPACITY - self % n_items ) then call error_raise ( err , ERROR_ALLOC , & \"vector_int64_t: append would exceed PIC_VECTOR_MAX_CAPACITY.\" ) return end if call vec_int64_grow ( self , self % n_items + n_new , err ) if ( self % n_items + n_new > vec_int64_capacity ( self )) return self % items ( self % n_items + 1 : self % n_items + n_new ) = values self % n_items = self % n_items + n_new end subroutine vec_int64_append","tags":"","url":"proc/vec_int64_append.html"},{"title":"vec_int64_at – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine vec_int64_at(self, index, value, err) Read element index , 1-based. ERROR_BOUNDS when out of range. Type Bound vector_int64_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( vector_int64_t ), intent(in) :: self integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: index integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Calls proc~~vec_int64_at~~CallsGraph proc~vec_int64_at vector_int64_t%vec_int64_at proc~error_raise error_raise proc~vec_int64_at->proc~error_raise proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~error_raise->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure subroutine vec_int64_at ( self , index , value , err ) !! Read element `index`, 1-based. `ERROR_BOUNDS` when out of range. class ( vector_int64_t ), intent ( in ) :: self integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: index integer ( int64 ), intent ( out ) :: value type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ), optional :: err if ( index < 1 . or . index > self % n_items ) then call error_raise ( err , ERROR_BOUNDS , \"vector_int64_t: at() index is out of range.\" ) return end if value = self % items ( index ) end subroutine vec_int64_at","tags":"","url":"proc/vec_int64_at.html"},{"title":"vec_int64_clear – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine vec_int64_clear(self) Drop every element but keep the allocated storage, so a vector reused\nacross iterations of an outer loop stops reallocating. Type Bound vector_int64_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( vector_int64_t ), intent(inout) :: self Source Code pure subroutine vec_int64_clear ( self ) !! Drop every element but keep the allocated storage, so a vector reused !! across iterations of an outer loop stops reallocating. class ( vector_int64_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self self % n_items = 0 end subroutine vec_int64_clear","tags":"","url":"proc/vec_int64_clear.html"},{"title":"vec_int64_destroy – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine vec_int64_destroy(self) Release all storage and reset to the empty state. Type Bound vector_int64_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( vector_int64_t ), intent(inout) :: self Source Code pure subroutine vec_int64_destroy ( self ) !! Release all storage and reset to the empty state. class ( vector_int64_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self self % n_items = 0 if ( allocated ( self % items )) deallocate ( self % items ) end subroutine vec_int64_destroy","tags":"","url":"proc/vec_int64_destroy.html"},{"title":"vec_int64_grow – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine vec_int64_grow(self, needed, err) Ensure capacity for at least needed elements, preserving the live\nones. Capacity starts at PIC_VECTOR_MIN_CAPACITY and doubles, which\nkeeps a run of push_back calls amortised O(1). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( vector_int64_t ), intent(inout) :: self integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: needed type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Calls proc~~vec_int64_grow~~CallsGraph proc~vec_int64_grow vec_int64_grow proc~error_raise error_raise proc~vec_int64_grow->proc~error_raise proc~vec_int64_capacity vector_int64_t%vec_int64_capacity proc~vec_int64_grow->proc~vec_int64_capacity proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~error_raise->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~vec_int64_grow~~CalledByGraph proc~vec_int64_grow vec_int64_grow proc~vec_int64_append vector_int64_t%vec_int64_append proc~vec_int64_append->proc~vec_int64_grow proc~vec_int64_push_back vector_int64_t%vec_int64_push_back proc~vec_int64_push_back->proc~vec_int64_grow proc~vec_int64_reserve vector_int64_t%vec_int64_reserve proc~vec_int64_reserve->proc~vec_int64_grow proc~vec_int64_resize vector_int64_t%vec_int64_resize proc~vec_int64_resize->proc~vec_int64_grow Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int64), private, allocatable :: bigger (:) integer(kind=default_int), private :: new_capacity integer, private :: stat Source Code pure subroutine vec_int64_grow ( self , needed , err ) !! Ensure capacity for at least `needed` elements, preserving the live !! ones. Capacity starts at PIC_VECTOR_MIN_CAPACITY and doubles, which !! keeps a run of `push_back` calls amortised O(1). class ( vector_int64_t ), intent ( inout ) :: self integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: needed type ( error_t ), intent ( inout ), optional :: err integer ( int64 ), allocatable :: bigger (:) integer ( default_int ) :: new_capacity integer :: stat if ( needed <= vec_int64_capacity ( self )) return ! `needed` is negative only if a caller's arithmetic wrapped on the way ! in. It cannot be compared against PIC_VECTOR_MAX_CAPACITY, which is ! `huge(0_default_int)` and so can never be exceeded by a value of that ! kind -- a guard written that way is dead code. The callers below check ! their headroom before adding; this catches anything that slips past. if ( needed < 0_default_int ) then call error_raise ( err , ERROR_ALLOC , & \"vector_int64_t: requested capacity overflowed.\" ) return end if new_capacity = max ( vec_int64_capacity ( self ), PIC_VECTOR_MIN_CAPACITY ) do while ( new_capacity < needed ) ! Doubling is done only when it cannot overflow default_int; past that ! point the clamp is the only remaining step, and `needed` is already ! known to fit. if ( new_capacity > PIC_VECTOR_MAX_CAPACITY / 2 ) then new_capacity = PIC_VECTOR_MAX_CAPACITY exit end if new_capacity = 2 * new_capacity end do allocate ( bigger ( new_capacity ), stat = stat ) ! `allocated` rather than `stat`: nvfortran does not set stat reliably ! for every element type, while `allocated` always reports the truth. if (. not . allocated ( bigger )) then call error_raise ( err , ERROR_ALLOC , \"vector_int64_t: allocation of backing storage failed.\" ) return end if if ( self % n_items > 0 ) bigger ( 1 : self % n_items ) = self % items ( 1 : self % n_items ) call move_alloc ( bigger , self % items ) end subroutine vec_int64_grow","tags":"","url":"proc/vec_int64_grow.html"},{"title":"vec_int64_pop_back – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine vec_int64_pop_back(self, value, err) Remove and return the last element. ERROR_BOUNDS when empty, in\nwhich case the vector is unchanged. Type Bound vector_int64_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( vector_int64_t ), intent(inout) :: self integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Calls proc~~vec_int64_pop_back~~CallsGraph proc~vec_int64_pop_back vector_int64_t%vec_int64_pop_back proc~error_raise error_raise proc~vec_int64_pop_back->proc~error_raise proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~error_raise->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key S