>proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_has_key hash_map_t%hash_map_has_key proc~hash_map_has_key->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_insert hash_map_t%hash_map_insert proc~hash_map_insert->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_remove hash_map_t%hash_map_remove proc~hash_map_remove->proc~find_entry Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: bucket integer(kind=default_int), private :: candidate integer(kind=default_int), private :: probe Source Code function find_entry ( this , key ) result ( entry ) !! Index of `key` in the insertion-order table, or 0 when absent. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: key integer ( default_int ) :: entry integer ( default_int ) :: bucket , probe , candidate entry = 0 if ( this % n_buckets == 0 ) return bucket = home_bucket ( this , key ) do probe = 1 , this % n_buckets candidate = this % buckets ( bucket ) if ( candidate == BUCKET_EMPTY ) return if ( key_matches ( this , candidate , key )) then entry = candidate return end if bucket = bucket + 1 if ( bucket > this % n_buckets ) bucket = 1 end do end function find_entry","tags":"","url":"proc/find_entry.html"},{"title":"hash_map_bucket_count – PIC","text":"private pure function hash_map_bucket_count(this) result(n) Current number of buckets, 0 while the map is unallocated. Exposed so\nthat callers and tests can observe growth; it never affects iteration\norder. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Source Code pure function hash_map_bucket_count ( this ) result ( n ) !! Current number of buckets, 0 while the map is unallocated. Exposed so !! that callers and tests can observe growth; it never affects iteration !! order. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( default_int ) :: n n = this % n_buckets end function hash_map_bucket_count","tags":"","url":"proc/hash_map_bucket_count.html"},{"title":"hash_map_has_key – PIC","text":"private  function hash_map_has_key(this, key) result(present_in_map) .true. when key is stored in the map. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this character(len=*), intent(in) :: key Return Value logical Calls proc~~hash_map_has_key~~CallsGraph proc~hash_map_has_key hash_map_t%hash_map_has_key proc~find_entry find_entry proc~hash_map_has_key->proc~find_entry proc~home_bucket home_bucket proc~find_entry->proc~home_bucket proc~key_matches key_matches proc~find_entry->proc~key_matches interface~fnv_1a_hash fnv_1a_hash proc~home_bucket->interface~fnv_1a_hash Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code function hash_map_has_key ( this , key ) result ( present_in_map ) !! `.true.` when `key` is stored in the map. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: key logical :: present_in_map present_in_map = ( find_entry ( this , key ) > 0 ) end function hash_map_has_key","tags":"","url":"proc/hash_map_has_key.html"},{"title":"hash_map_is_empty – PIC","text":"private pure function hash_map_is_empty(this) result(empty) .true. when the map holds no entries. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value logical Source Code pure function hash_map_is_empty ( this ) result ( empty ) !! `.true.` when the map holds no entries. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this logical :: empty empty = ( this % n_entries == 0 ) end function hash_map_is_empty","tags":"","url":"proc/hash_map_is_empty.html"},{"title":"hash_map_keys – PIC","text":"private  function hash_map_keys(this) result(res) All keys, in insertion order. Fortran arrays are not ragged, so every element has the same length:\nkeys shorter than the longest one are blank-padded on the right. That\npadding is lossy for keys that themselves end in blanks, and a\nzero-length key comes back as a blank string. Use at() when the\nexact key text matters. The result is zero-sized for an empty map. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value character(len=:), allocatable, (:) Calls proc~~hash_map_keys~~CallsGraph proc~hash_map_keys hash_map_t%hash_map_keys proc~entry_key entry_key proc~hash_map_keys->proc~entry_key Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=default_int), private :: longest Source Code function hash_map_keys ( this ) result ( res ) !! All keys, in insertion order. !! !! Fortran arrays are not ragged, so every element has the same length: !! keys shorter than the longest one are blank-padded on the right. That !! padding is lossy for keys that themselves end in blanks, and a !! zero-length key comes back as a blank string. Use `at()` when the !! exact key text matters. The result is zero-sized for an empty map. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this character ( len = :), allocatable :: res (:) integer ( default_int ) :: i , longest longest = 1 do i = 1 , this % n_entries if ( this % key_size ( i ) > longest ) longest = this % key_size ( i ) end do allocate ( character ( len = longest ) :: res ( this % n_entries )) do i = 1 , this % n_entries res ( i ) = entry_key ( this , i ) end do end function hash_map_keys","tags":"","url":"proc/hash_map_keys.html"},{"title":"hash_map_size – PIC","text":"private pure function hash_map_size(this) result(n) Number of entries currently stored. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Source Code pure function hash_map_size ( this ) result ( n ) !! Number of entries currently stored. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( default_int ) :: n n = this % n_entries end function hash_map_size","tags":"","url":"proc/hash_map_size.html"},{"title":"hash_map_values – PIC","text":"private  function hash_map_values(this) result(res) All values, in insertion order. values() and keys() are index\naligned: values(i) belongs to keys(i) . Zero-sized for an empty\nmap. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value integer(kind=int64), allocatable, (:) Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i Source Code function hash_map_values ( this ) result ( res ) !! All values, in insertion order. `values()` and `keys()` are index !! aligned: `values(i)` belongs to `keys(i)`. Zero-sized for an empty !! map. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( int64 ), allocatable :: res (:) integer ( default_int ) :: i allocate ( res ( this % n_entries )) do i = 1 , this % n_entries res ( i ) = this % entry_value ( i ) end do end function hash_map_values","tags":"","url":"proc/hash_map_values.html"},{"title":"home_bucket – PIC","text":"private  function home_bucket(this, key) result(bucket) Bucket a key hashes to before probing. fnv_1a_hash returns a fixed-width int32 hash code; that width is\nan algorithmic property of FNV-1a and is intentionally not default_int . The code is widened to int64 and masked with n_buckets - 1 , which is valid because the bucket count is always a\npower of two, and which discards the sign bits along with the high\nbits. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this character(len=*), intent(in) :: key Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Calls proc~~home_bucket~~CallsGraph proc~home_bucket home_bucket interface~fnv_1a_hash fnv_1a_hash proc~home_bucket->interface~fnv_1a_hash Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~home_bucket~~CalledByGraph proc~home_bucket home_bucket proc~find_entry find_entry proc~find_entry->proc~home_bucket proc~probe_free probe_free proc~probe_free->proc~home_bucket proc~hash_map_get hash_map_t%hash_map_get proc~hash_map_get->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_has_key hash_map_t%hash_map_has_key proc~hash_map_has_key->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_insert hash_map_t%hash_map_insert proc~hash_map_insert->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_insert->proc~probe_free proc~grow_buckets grow_buckets proc~hash_map_insert->proc~grow_buckets proc~hash_map_remove hash_map_t%hash_map_remove proc~hash_map_remove->proc~find_entry proc~rebuild_buckets rebuild_buckets proc~hash_map_remove->proc~rebuild_buckets proc~rebuild_buckets->proc~probe_free proc~grow_buckets->proc~rebuild_buckets Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int32), private :: code Source Code function home_bucket ( this , key ) result ( bucket ) !! Bucket a key hashes to before probing. !! !! `fnv_1a_hash` returns a fixed-width `int32` hash code; that width is !! an algorithmic property of FNV-1a and is intentionally *not* !! `default_int`. The code is widened to `int64` and masked with !! `n_buckets - 1`, which is valid because the bucket count is always a !! power of two, and which discards the sign bits along with the high !! bits. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: key integer ( default_int ) :: bucket integer ( int32 ) :: code code = fnv_1a_hash ( key ) bucket = int ( iand ( int ( code , int64 ), int ( this % n_buckets - 1 , int64 )), default_int ) + 1 end function home_bucket","tags":"","url":"proc/home_bucket.html"},{"title":"key_matches – PIC","text":"private  function key_matches(this, entry, key) result(same) Exact key comparison: same length and same characters. Deliberately\nnot the Fortran == operator, which blank-pads the shorter operand. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: entry character(len=*), intent(in) :: key Return Value logical Called by proc~~key_matches~~CalledByGraph proc~key_matches key_matches proc~find_entry find_entry proc~find_entry->proc~key_matches proc~hash_map_get hash_map_t%hash_map_get proc~hash_map_get->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_has_key hash_map_t%hash_map_has_key proc~hash_map_has_key->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_insert hash_map_t%hash_map_insert proc~hash_map_insert->proc~find_entry proc~hash_map_remove hash_map_t%hash_map_remove proc~hash_map_remove->proc~find_entry Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i Source Code function key_matches ( this , entry , key ) result ( same ) !! Exact key comparison: same length and same characters. Deliberately !! not the Fortran `==` operator, which blank-pads the shorter operand. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: entry character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: key logical :: same integer ( default_int ) :: i same = . false . if ( this % key_size ( entry ) /= int ( len ( key ), default_int )) return do i = 1 , this % key_size ( entry ) if ( this % key_pool ( this % key_start ( entry ) + i - 1 ) /= key ( i : i )) return end do same = . true . end function key_matches","tags":"","url":"proc/key_matches.html"},{"title":"probe_free – PIC","text":"private  function probe_free(this, key) result(bucket) First empty bucket in the probe sequence of key . The load factor\nkeeps at least one bucket empty at all times, so this always finds\none. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this character(len=*), intent(in) :: key Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Calls proc~~probe_free~~CallsGraph proc~probe_free probe_free proc~home_bucket home_bucket proc~probe_free->proc~home_bucket interface~fnv_1a_hash fnv_1a_hash proc~home_bucket->interface~fnv_1a_hash Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~probe_free~~CalledByGraph proc~probe_free probe_free proc~hash_map_insert hash_map_t%hash_map_insert proc~hash_map_insert->proc~probe_free proc~grow_buckets grow_buckets proc~hash_map_insert->proc~grow_buckets proc~rebuild_buckets rebuild_buckets proc~rebuild_buckets->proc~probe_free proc~grow_buckets->proc~rebuild_buckets proc~hash_map_remove hash_map_t%hash_map_remove proc~hash_map_remove->proc~rebuild_buckets Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: probe Source Code function probe_free ( this , key ) result ( bucket ) !! First empty bucket in the probe sequence of `key`. The load factor !! keeps at least one bucket empty at all times, so this always finds !! one. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: key integer ( default_int ) :: bucket integer ( default_int ) :: probe bucket = home_bucket ( this , key ) do probe = 1 , this % n_buckets if ( this % buckets ( bucket ) == BUCKET_EMPTY ) return bucket = bucket + 1 if ( bucket > this % n_buckets ) bucket = 1 end do end function probe_free","tags":"","url":"proc/probe_free.html"},{"title":"grow_buckets – PIC","text":"private  subroutine grow_buckets(this) Double the bucket table and rehash. Entry storage is never moved. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(inout) :: this Calls proc~~grow_buckets~~CallsGraph proc~grow_buckets grow_buckets proc~rebuild_buckets rebuild_buckets proc~grow_buckets->proc~rebuild_buckets proc~entry_key entry_key proc~rebuild_buckets->proc~entry_key proc~probe_free probe_free proc~rebuild_buckets->proc~probe_free proc~home_bucket home_bucket proc~probe_free->proc~home_bucket interface~fnv_1a_hash fnv_1a_hash proc~home_bucket->interface~fnv_1a_hash Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~grow_buckets~~CalledByGraph proc~grow_buckets grow_buckets proc~hash_map_insert hash_map_t%hash_map_insert proc~hash_map_insert->proc~grow_buckets Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private, allocatable :: fresh (:) Source Code subroutine grow_buckets ( this ) !! Double the bucket table and rehash. Entry storage is never moved. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this integer ( default_int ), allocatable :: fresh (:) allocate ( fresh ( this % n_buckets * 2 )) deallocate ( this % buckets ) call move_alloc ( fresh , this % buckets ) this % n_buckets = this % n_buckets * 2 call rebuild_buckets ( this ) end subroutine grow_buckets","tags":"","url":"proc/grow_buckets.html"},{"title":"hash_map_at – PIC","text":"private  subroutine hash_map_at(this, position, key, value, err) Fetch the entry at position in the iteration order, counting from 1.\nThis is the exact-fidelity iteration accessor: key comes back with\nthe key’s true length, trailing blanks included. An out-of-range position yields key == \"\" , value == 0 and ERROR_VALIDATION in err when it is present. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(in) :: this integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: position character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: key integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(out), optional :: err Calls proc~~hash_map_at~~CallsGraph proc~hash_map_at hash_map_t%hash_map_at proc~entry_key entry_key proc~hash_map_at->proc~entry_key proc~error_set error_t%error_set proc~hash_map_at->proc~error_set Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine hash_map_at ( this , position , key , value , err ) !! Fetch the entry at `position` in the iteration order, counting from 1. !! This is the exact-fidelity iteration accessor: `key` comes back with !! the key's true length, trailing blanks included. !! !! An out-of-range `position` yields `key == \"\"`, `value == 0` and !! `ERROR_VALIDATION` in `err` when it is present. class ( hash_map_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: position character ( len = :), allocatable , intent ( out ) :: key integer ( int64 ), intent ( out ) :: value type ( error_t ), intent ( out ), optional :: err if ( position < 1 . or . position > this % n_entries ) then key = \"\" value = 0_int64 if ( present ( err )) then call err % set ( ERROR_VALIDATION , \"pic_hash_map: iteration index out of range\" ) end if return end if key = entry_key ( this , position ) value = this % entry_value ( position ) end subroutine hash_map_at","tags":"","url":"proc/hash_map_at.html"},{"title":"hash_map_clear – PIC","text":"private  subroutine hash_map_clear(this) Remove every entry but keep the allocated capacity, so that refilling\nthe map does not have to reallocate. A cleared map iterates as empty\nand the next insert starts a fresh insertion order at position 1. Type Bound hash_map_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( hash_map_t ), intent(inout) :: this Source Code s