hide_cursor","tags":"","url":"proc/ansi_hide_cursor.html"},{"title":"ansi_move_to – PIC","text":"public pure function ansi_move_to(row, col) result(seq) Move the cursor to a one-based row and column. Numbers are converted with to_string , never with internal I/O: a write to an internal unit is not allowed in a pure procedure, and\nit would drag in locale-dependent formatting for no gain. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: row One-based row; values below 1 are clamped to 1. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: col One-based column; values below 1 are clamped to 1. Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Calls proc~~ansi_move_to~~CallsGraph proc~ansi_move_to ansi_move_to interface~to_string to_string proc~ansi_move_to->interface~to_string Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~ansi_move_to~~CalledByGraph proc~ansi_move_to ansi_move_to proc~frame_render frame_t%frame_render proc~frame_render->proc~ansi_move_to Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function ansi_move_to ( row , col ) result ( seq ) !! Move the cursor to a one-based row and column. !! !! Numbers are converted with `to_string`, never with internal I/O: a !! `write` to an internal unit is not allowed in a `pure` procedure, and !! it would drag in locale-dependent formatting for no gain. integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: row !! One-based row; values below 1 are clamped to 1. integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: col !! One-based column; values below 1 are clamped to 1. character ( len = :), allocatable :: seq seq = CSI // to_string ( int ( max ( 1_default_int , row ), int32 )) // \";\" // & to_string ( int ( max ( 1_default_int , col ), int32 )) // \"H\" end function ansi_move_to","tags":"","url":"proc/ansi_move_to.html"},{"title":"ansi_reset – PIC","text":"public pure function ansi_reset() result(seq) Reset every attribute. Arguments None Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Called by proc~~ansi_reset~~CalledByGraph proc~ansi_reset ansi_reset proc~styled styled proc~styled->proc~ansi_reset Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function ansi_reset () result ( seq ) !! Reset every attribute. character ( len = :), allocatable :: seq seq = CSI // \"0m\" end function ansi_reset","tags":"","url":"proc/ansi_reset.html"},{"title":"ansi_reverse – PIC","text":"public pure function ansi_reverse() result(seq) Swap foreground and background. Arguments None Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Source Code pure function ansi_reverse () result ( seq ) !! Swap foreground and background. character ( len = :), allocatable :: seq seq = CSI // \"7m\" end function ansi_reverse","tags":"","url":"proc/ansi_reverse.html"},{"title":"ansi_show_cursor – PIC","text":"public pure function ansi_show_cursor() result(seq) Show the cursor. Arguments None Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Source Code pure function ansi_show_cursor () result ( seq ) !! Show the cursor. character ( len = :), allocatable :: seq seq = CSI // \"?25h\" end function ansi_show_cursor","tags":"","url":"proc/ansi_show_cursor.html"},{"title":"display_width – PIC","text":"public pure function display_width(text) result(width) Number of columns text occupies. Counts UTF-8 code points and skips ANSI escape sequences, which take\nno space on screen. Continuation bytes, those with the top two bits 10 , do not start a character and so do not count. A byte that is\nnot valid UTF-8 counts as one, so invalid input degrades to the byte\ncount rather than to something arbitrary. One column per code point: an East Asian wide character occupies two\nand is counted once here. Getting that right needs a width table, and\nis out of scope. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text Text to measure. Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Calls proc~~display_width~~CallsGraph proc~display_width display_width proc~escape_length escape_length proc~display_width->proc~escape_length Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: byte integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=default_int), private :: skip Source Code pure function display_width ( text ) result ( width ) !! Number of columns `text` occupies. !! !! Counts UTF-8 code points and skips ANSI escape sequences, which take !! no space on screen. Continuation bytes, those with the top two bits !! `10`, do not start a character and so do not count. A byte that is !! not valid UTF-8 counts as one, so invalid input degrades to the byte !! count rather than to something arbitrary. !! !! One column per code point: an East Asian wide character occupies two !! and is counted once here. Getting that right needs a width table, and !! is out of scope. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: text !! Text to measure. integer ( default_int ) :: width integer ( default_int ) :: i , byte , skip width = 0_default_int i = 1_default_int do while ( i <= len ( text , kind = default_int )) skip = escape_length ( text , i ) if ( skip > 0_default_int ) then i = i + skip cycle end if byte = iachar ( text ( i : i ), default_int ) if ( byte < 128_default_int . or . byte >= 192_default_int ) then width = width + 1_default_int end if i = i + 1_default_int end do end function display_width","tags":"","url":"proc/display_width.html"},{"title":"styled – PIC","text":"public pure function styled(text, fg, bg, bold) result(seq) Wrap text in the requested attributes and a reset. The reset is emitted only when something was actually set, so styled(\"x\") is \"x\" and not \"x\" with a stray reset after it. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text Text to wrap. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional :: fg Foreground colour, one of the ANSI_* constants. integer(kind=int32), intent(in), optional :: bg Background colour, one of the ANSI_* constants. logical, intent(in), optional :: bold Whether to embolden. Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Calls proc~~styled~~CallsGraph proc~styled styled proc~ansi_bg ansi_bg proc~styled->proc~ansi_bg proc~ansi_bold ansi_bold proc~styled->proc~ansi_bold proc~ansi_fg ansi_fg proc~styled->proc~ansi_fg proc~ansi_reset ansi_reset proc~styled->proc~ansi_reset interface~to_string to_string proc~ansi_bg->interface~to_string proc~base_code base_code proc~ansi_bg->proc~base_code proc~ansi_fg->interface~to_string proc~ansi_fg->proc~base_code Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial logical, private :: any_set Source Code pure function styled ( text , fg , bg , bold ) result ( seq ) !! Wrap `text` in the requested attributes and a reset. !! !! The reset is emitted only when something was actually set, so !! `styled(\"x\")` is `\"x\"` and not `\"x\"` with a stray reset after it. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: text !! Text to wrap. integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ), optional :: fg !! Foreground colour, one of the ANSI_* constants. integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ), optional :: bg !! Background colour, one of the ANSI_* constants. logical , intent ( in ), optional :: bold !! Whether to embolden. character ( len = :), allocatable :: seq logical :: any_set seq = \"\" any_set = . false . if ( present ( fg )) then seq = seq // ansi_fg ( fg ) any_set = . true . end if if ( present ( bg )) then seq = seq // ansi_bg ( bg ) any_set = . true . end if if ( present ( bold )) then if ( bold ) then seq = seq // ansi_bold () any_set = . true . end if end if seq = seq // text if ( any_set ) seq = seq // ansi_reset () end function styled","tags":"","url":"proc/styled.html"},{"title":"truncate_to_width – PIC","text":"public pure function truncate_to_width(text, width) result(cut) Cut text to at most width columns, never mid-character and never\ninside an escape sequence. Escape sequences are kept and cost nothing, so a styled row keeps its\ncolours and is cut by what it shows rather than by how it is spelled.\nNote that a cut can leave an attribute set with its reset dropped; ansi_reset() after writing a truncated row settles that. A width of zero still keeps nothing at all, escape sequences\nincluded: a caller asking for no columns wants no bytes. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text Text to truncate. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: width Maximum width in columns. Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Calls proc~~truncate_to_width~~CallsGraph proc~truncate_to_width truncate_to_width proc~escape_length escape_length proc~truncate_to_width->proc~escape_length Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Called by proc~~truncate_to_width~~CalledByGraph proc~truncate_to_width truncate_to_width proc~frame_set_line frame_t%frame_set_line proc~frame_set_line->proc~truncate_to_width Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: byte integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=default_int), private :: last integer(kind=default_int), private :: seen integer(kind=default_int), private :: skip Source Code pure function truncate_to_width ( text , width ) result ( cut ) !! Cut `text` to at most `width` columns, never mid-character and never !! inside an escape sequence. !! !! Escape sequences are kept and cost nothing, so a styled row keeps its !! colours and is cut by what it shows rather than by how it is spelled. !! Note that a cut can leave an attribute set with its reset dropped; !! `ansi_reset()` after writing a truncated row settles that. !! !! A `width` of zero still keeps nothing at all, escape sequences !! included: a caller asking for no columns wants no bytes. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: text !! Text to truncate. integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: width !! Maximum width in columns. character ( len = :), allocatable :: cut integer ( default_int ) :: i , byte , seen , skip , last if ( width <= 0_default_int ) then cut = \"\" return end if seen = 0_default_int last = len ( text , kind = default_int ) i = 1_default_int do while ( i <= last ) skip = escape_length ( text , i ) if ( skip > 0_default_int ) then i = i + skip cycle end if byte = iachar ( text ( i : i ), default_int ) if ( byte < 128_default_int . or . byte >= 192_default_int ) then if ( seen == width ) then cut = text ( 1 : i - 1_default_int ) return end if seen = seen + 1_default_int end if i = i + 1_default_int end do cut = text end function truncate_to_width","tags":"","url":"proc/truncate_to_width.html"},{"title":"base_code – PIC","text":"private pure function base_code(color, base) result(code) SGR number for a named colour: base + n , or base + 60 + n bright. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: color integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: base 30 for foreground, 40 for background. Return Value integer(kind=int32) Called by proc~~base_code~~CalledByGraph proc~base_code base_code proc~ansi_bg ansi_bg proc~ansi_bg->proc~base_code proc~ansi_fg ansi_fg proc~ansi_fg->proc~base_code proc~styled styled proc~styled->proc~ansi_bg proc~styled->proc~ansi_fg Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int32), private :: clamped Source Code pure function base_code ( color , base ) result ( code ) !! SGR number for a named colour: `base + n`, or `base + 60 + n` bright. integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ) :: color integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ) :: base !! 30 for foreground, 40 for background. integer ( int32 ) :: code integer ( int32 ) :: clamped clamped = max ( 0_int32 , min ( 15_int32 , color )) if ( clamped < 8_int32 ) then code = base + clamped else code = base + 60_int32 + ( clamped - 8_int32 ) end if end function base_code","tags":"","url":"proc/base_code.html"},{"title":"clamp_byte – PIC","text":"private pure function clamp_byte(value) result(r) Clamp to 0 to 255, so a caller’s arithmetic slip cannot emit a\nsequence the terminal will misparse. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: value Return Value integer(kind=int32) Called by proc~~clamp_byte~~CalledByGraph proc~clamp_byte clamp_byte proc~ansi_bg_256 ansi_bg_256 proc~ansi_bg_256->proc~clamp_byte proc~ansi_bg_rgb ansi_bg_rgb proc~ansi_bg_rgb->proc~clamp_byte proc~ansi_fg_256 ansi_fg_256 proc~ansi_fg_256->proc~clamp_byte proc~ansi_fg_rgb ansi_fg_rgb proc~ansi_fg_rgb->proc~clamp_byte Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure function clamp_byte ( value ) result ( r ) !! Clamp to 0 to 255, so a caller's arithmetic slip cannot emit a !! sequence the terminal will misparse. integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ) :: value integer ( int32 ) :: r r = max ( 0_int32 , min ( 255_int32 , value )) end function clamp_byte","tags":"","url":"proc/clamp_byte.html"},{"title":"escape_length – PIC","text":"private pure function escape_length(text, start) result(n) Length of the escape sequence beginning at start , or 0. Escape sequences occupy no columns, so measuring and truncating both\nhave to step over them rather than count them. Without this, styled(\"ARRIVALS\", fg=ANSI_RED) measures seventeen wide rather than\neight, and truncating a styled row could cut an escape sequence in\nhalf – which does not merely lose the colour, it leaves the terminal\nreading the rest of the row as parameters. Recognises the CSI form, ESC [ then parameter and intermediate\nbytes then a final byte in the range @ to ~ , which is what\neverything in this module emits. An unterminated sequence swallows\nthe remaining text, since there is no position in it that could\nsafely be cut. Any other ESC pair counts as two bytes. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text Text being scanned. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: start Index to examine. Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Called by proc~~escape_length~~CalledByGraph proc~escape_length escape_length proc~display_width display_width proc~display_width->proc~escape_length proc~truncate_to_width truncate_to_width proc~truncate_to_width->proc~escape_length proc~frame_set_line frame_t%frame_set_line proc~frame_set_line->proc~truncate_to_width Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: byte integer(kind=default_int), private :: j integer(kind=default_int), private :: last Source Code pure function escape_length ( text , start ) result ( n ) !! Length of the escape sequence beginning at `start`, or 0. !! !! Escape sequences occupy no columns, so measuring and truncating both !! have to step over them rather than count them. Without this, !! `styled(\"ARRIVALS\", fg=ANSI_RED)` measures seventeen wide rather than !! eight, and truncating a styled row could cut an escape sequence in !! half -- which does not merely lose the colour, it leaves the terminal !! reading the rest of the row as parameters. !! !! Recognises the CSI form, `ESC [` then parameter and intermediate !! bytes then a final byte in the range `@` to `~`, which is what !! everything in this module emits. An unterminated sequence swallows !! the remaining text, since there is no position in it that could !! safely be cut. Any other `ESC` pair counts as two bytes. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: text !! Text being scanned. integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: start !! Index to examine. integer ( default_int ) :: n integer ( default_int ) :: j , last , byte n = 0_default_int last = len ( text , kind = default_int ) if ( start > last ) return if ( text ( start : start ) /= ESC ) return if ( start == last ) then n = 1_default_int ! a lone trailing ESC return end if if ( text ( start + 1_default_int : start + 1_default_int ) /= \"[\" ) then n = 2_default_int ! ESC followed by something else return end if j = start + 2_default_int do while ( j <= last ) byte = iachar ( text ( j : j ), default_int ) if ( byte >= 64_default_int . and . byte <= 126_default_int ) then n = j - start + 1_default_int ! final byte, sequence complete return end if if ( byte < 32_default_int . or . byte > 63_default_int ) then ! A byte that is neither parameter, intermediate nor final aborts ! the sequence where it stands -- which is what a terminal does ! with it. Everything from here on is ordinary text again. n = j - start return end if j = j + 1_default_int end do n = last - start + 1_default_int ! ran off the end; nowhere safe to cut end function escape_length","tags":"","url":"proc/escape_length.html"},{"title":"frame_col_count – PIC","text":"private pure function frame_col_count(this) result(c) Width the frame truncates to. Type Bound frame_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( frame_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Source Code pure function frame_col_count ( this ) result ( c ) !! Width the frame truncates to. class ( frame_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( default_int ) :: c c = this % cols end function frame_col_count","tags":"","url":"proc/frame_col_count.html"},{"title":"frame_render – PIC","text":"private  function frame_render(this) result(output) The escape sequence that brings the screen up to date. Only rows whose text changed since the last render are emitted, each\nas a move, a line clear and the text. One string comes back and the\ncaller writes it once: a single write cannot tear, where a write per\nrow can. Type Bound frame_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( frame_t ), intent(inout) :: this Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Calls proc~~frame_render~~CallsGraph proc~frame_render frame_t%frame_render proc~ansi_clear_line ansi_clear_line proc~frame_render->proc~ansi_clear_line proc~ansi_move_to ansi_move_to proc~frame_render->proc~ansi_move_to interface~to_string to_string proc~ansi_move_to->interface~to_string Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: i Source Code function frame_render ( this ) result ( output ) !! The escape sequence that brings the screen up to date. !! !! Only rows whose text changed since the last render are emitted, each !! as a move, a line clear and the text. One string comes back and the !! caller writes it once: a single write cannot tear, where a write per !! row can. class ( frame_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this character ( len = :), allocatable :: output integer ( default_int ) :: i output = \"\" if ( this % rows == 0_default_int ) then this % full_redraw = . false . return end if do i = 1_default_int , this % rows if (. not . this % full_redraw ) then ! Compared through `char` with an explicit length test. ! `string_type`'s `==` is Fortran character comparison, which pads ! the shorter side with blanks, so \"abc\" and \"abc   \" compare ! equal. Those are the same text but not the same row: with a ! background colour still open at the end of the line, the three ! extra cells are painted. if ( len ( char ( this % current ( i ))) == len ( char ( this % shown ( i )))) then if ( char ( this % current ( i )) == char ( this % shown ( i ))) cycle end if end if output = output // ansi_move_to ( i , 1_default_int ) // ansi_clear_line () // & char ( this % current ( i )) this % shown ( i ) = this % current ( i ) end do this % full_redraw = . false . end function frame_render","tags":"","url":"proc/frame_render.html"},{"title":"frame_row_count – PIC","text":"private pure function frame_row_count(this) result(r) Number of lines the frame holds. Type Bound frame_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( frame_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Source Code pure function frame_row_count ( this ) result ( r ) !! Number of lines the frame holds. class ( frame_t ), intent ( in ) :: this integer ( default_int ) :: r r = this % rows end function frame_row_count","tags":"","url":"proc/frame_row_count.html"},{"title":"pending_is_empty – PIC","text":"private pure function pending_is_empty(this) result(r) Whether a partial sequence is being carried. Type Bound pending_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( pending_t ), intent(in) :: this Return Value logical Source Code pure function pending_is_empty ( this ) result ( r ) !! Whether a partial sequence is being carried. class ( pending_t ), intent ( in ) :: this logical :: r r = this % length == 0_default_int end function pending_is_empty","tags":"","url":"proc/pending_is_empty.html"},{"title":"decode_keys – PIC","text":"public pure subroutine decode_keys(pending, bytes, events, n_events) Decode raw terminal bytes into key events. A state machine rather than a lookup, because escape sequences arrive\nin whatever chunks the terminal and the kernel decide on: ESC [ A may come as one read or three. Anything that ends mid-sequence is\nkept in pending and completed by the next call. A lone ESC – the user pressing Escape, as opposed to the start of\na sequence – is indistinguishable from a truncated sequence until\nmore bytes arrive or do not. It is held in pending , and reported as KEY_ESC on the next call if nothing followed it. That is one read’s\nworth of latency on the Escape key and no ambiguity, which is the\ntrade every terminal program makes. Multi-byte UTF-8 is passed through as individual KEY_CHAR bytes; a\ncaller that wants code points reassembles them. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pending_t ), intent(inout) :: pending Carried partial sequence; pass the same one every call. character(len=*), intent(in) :: bytes Bytes just read from the terminal. May be empty, which is how a\ncaller flushes a held ESC . type( key_event_t ), intent(out) :: events (:) Decoded events, filled from 1. integer(kind=default_int), intent(out) :: n_events How many entries of events were filled. Calls proc~~decode_keys~~CallsGraph proc~decode_keys decode_keys proc~decode_single decode_single proc~decode_keys->proc~decode_single proc~emit emit proc~decode_keys->proc~emit proc~match_sequence match_sequence proc~decode_keys->proc~match_sequence proc~pending_clear pending_t%pending_clear proc~decode_keys->proc~pending_clear Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial logical, private :: complete integer(kind=default_int), private :: consumed type( key_event_t ), private :: event integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=default_int), private :: n integer(kind=default_int), private :: used character(len=PENDING_CAPACITY), private :: work Source Code pure subroutine decode_keys ( pending , bytes , events , n_events ) !! Decode raw terminal bytes into key events. !! !! A state machine rather than a lookup, because escape sequences arrive !! in whatever chunks the terminal and the kernel decide on: `ESC [ A` !! may come as one read or three. Anything that ends mid-sequence is !! kept in `pending` and completed by the next call. !! !! A lone `ESC` -- the user pressing Escape, as opposed to the start of !! a sequence -- is indistinguishable from a truncated sequence until !! more bytes arrive or do not. It is held in `pending`, and reported as !! `KEY_ESC` on the next call if nothing followed it. That is one read's !! worth of latency on the Escape key and no ambiguity, which is the !! trade every terminal program makes. !! !! Multi-byte UTF-8 is passed through as individual `KEY_CHAR` bytes; a !! caller that wants code points reassembles them. type ( pending_t ), intent ( inout ) :: pending !! Carried partial sequence; pass the same one every call. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: bytes !! Bytes just read from the terminal. May be empty, which is how a !! caller flushes a held `ESC`. type ( key_event_t ), intent ( out ) :: events (:) !! Decoded events, filled from 1. integer ( default_int ), intent ( out ) :: n_events !! How many entries of `events` were filled. character ( len = PENDING_CAPACITY + 1 ) :: work integer ( default_int ) :: used , i , n , consumed type ( key_event_t ) :: event logical :: complete n_events = 0_default_int ! An empty read with a held ESC means the user really did press Escape: ! nothing followed it, so it cannot be the start of a sequence. if ( len ( bytes ) == 0 ) then if ( pending % length > 0_default_int ) then if ( pending % buffer ( 1 : 1 ) == ESC . and . pending % length == 1_default_int ) then call emit ( events , n_events , KEY_ESC , 0_int32 ) end if call pending % clear () end if return end if used = pending % length work = \"\" if ( used > 0_default_int ) work ( 1 : used ) = pending % buffer ( 1 : used ) n = len ( bytes , kind = default_int ) i = 1_default_int do while ( i <= n ) if ( used == 0_default_int . and . bytes ( i : i ) /= ESC ) then ! the common case: an ordinary byte, no sequence in progress call decode_single ( iachar ( bytes ( i : i ), default_int ), event ) call emit ( events , n_events , event % code , event % char_code ) i = i + 1_default_int cycle end if ! inside a sequence: accumulate and test for completeness if ( used >= PENDING_CAPACITY ) then ! Not a sequence we know, and too long to be one. Drop it rather ! than grow without bound, and resynchronise on the next byte. used = 0_default_int cycle end if used = used + 1_default_int work ( used : used ) = bytes ( i : i ) i = i + 1_default_int call match_sequence ( work ( 1 : used ), complete , event , consumed ) if ( complete ) then if ( event % code /= KEY_NONE ) call emit ( events , n_events , event % code , event % char_code ) used = used - consumed ! A leftover can only be the byte appended a moment ago, since ! `match_sequence` consumes everything else it decides on. Put it ! back in the input rather than leaving it in the buffer, so the ! next turn classifies it from scratch -- it may be an ESC opening ! a new sequence. if ( used > 0_default_int ) then i = i - used used = 0_default_int end if end if end do call pending % clear () if ( used > 0_default_int ) then pending % buffer ( 1 : used ) = work ( 1 : used ) pending % length = used end if end subroutine decode_keys","tags":"","url":"proc/decode_keys.html"},{"title":"decode_single – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine decode_single(byte, event) Decode one byte that is not part of an escape sequence. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: byte iachar value of the byte. type( key_event_t ), intent(out) :: event Called by proc~~decode_single~~CalledByGraph proc~decode_single decode_single proc~decode_keys decode_keys proc~decode_keys->proc~decode_single Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure subroutine decode_single ( byte , event ) !! Decode one byte that is not part of an escape sequence. integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: byte !! `iachar` value of the byte. type ( key_event_t ), intent ( out ) :: event event % char_code = 0_int32 select case ( byte ) case ( 13 , 10 ) event % code = KEY_ENTER case ( 127 , 8 ) event % code = KEY_BACKSPACE case ( 9 ) event % code = KEY_TAB case ( 3 ) event % code = KEY_CTRL_C case default event % code = KEY_CHAR event % char_code = int ( byte , int32 ) end select end subroutine decode_single","tags":"","url":"proc/decode_single.html"},{"title":"emit – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine emit(events, n_events, code, char_code) Append an event, silently dropping any beyond the caller’s array. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( key_event_t ), intent(inout) :: events (:) integer(kind=default_int), intent(inout) :: n_events integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: code integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: char_code Called by proc~~emit~~CalledByGraph proc~emit emit proc~decode_keys decode_keys proc~decode_keys->proc~emit Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure subroutine emit ( events , n_events , code , char_code ) !! Append an event, silently dropping any beyond the caller's array. type ( key_event_t ), intent ( inout ) :: events (:) integer ( default_int ), intent ( inout ) :: n_events integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ) :: code integer ( int32 ), intent ( in ) :: char_code if ( n_events >= size ( events , kind = default_int )) return n_events = n_events + 1_default_int events ( n_events )% code = code events ( n_events )% char_code = char_code end subroutine emit","tags":"","url":"proc/emit.html"},{"title":"frame_invalidate – PIC","text":"private  subroutine frame_invalidate(this) Force the next render to emit every row. For when something outside the frame has written to the screen, so\nthe record of what is displayed can no longer be trusted. Type Bound frame_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( frame_t ), intent(inout) :: this Source Code subroutine frame_invalidate ( this ) !! Force the next `render` to emit every row. !! !! For when something outside the frame has written to the screen, so !! the record of what is displayed can no longer be trusted. class ( frame_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this this % full_redraw = . true . end subroutine frame_invalidate","tags":"","url":"proc/frame_invalidate.html"},{"title":"frame_resize – PIC","text":"private  subroutine frame_resize(this, rows, cols) Set the frame’s dimensions, discarding the diff. A resize invalidates the previous frame: the terminal has thrown away\nwhat was on screen, so nothing can be assumed still to be there. Type Bound frame_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( frame_t ), intent(inout) :: this integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: rows Number of lines; below 0 is treated as 0. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: cols Width in code points; below 0 is treated as 0. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: c integer(kind=default_int), private :: i integer(kind=default_int), private :: r Source Code subroutine frame_resize ( this , rows , cols ) !! Set the frame's dimensions, discarding the diff. !! !! A resize invalidates the previous frame: the terminal has thrown away !! what was on screen, so nothing can be assumed still to be there. class ( frame_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: rows !! Number of lines; below 0 is treated as 0. integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: cols !! Width in code points; below 0 is treated as 0. integer ( default_int ) :: r , c , i r = max ( 0_default_int , rows ) c = max ( 0_default_int , cols ) if ( allocated ( this % current )) deallocate ( this % current ) if ( allocated ( this % shown )) deallocate ( this % shown ) this % rows = r this % cols = c allocate ( this % current ( r )) allocate ( this % shown ( r )) do i = 1_default_int , r this % current ( i ) = \"\" this % shown ( i ) = \"\" end do this % full_redraw = . true . end subroutine frame_resize","tags":"","url":"proc/frame_resize.html"},{"title":"frame_set_line – PIC","text":"private  subroutine frame_set_line(this, row, text) Set one line, truncated to the frame width in code points. A row outside the frame is ignored rather than an error: a caller\ndrawing a list longer than the terminal should not have to check\nevery write. Type Bound frame_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( frame_t ), intent(inout) :: this integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: row One-based row. character(len=*), intent(in) :: text Text for the row. Calls proc~~frame_set_line~~CallsGraph proc~frame_set_line frame_t%frame_set_line proc~truncate_to_width truncate_to_width proc~frame_set_line->proc~truncate_to_width proc~escape_length escape_length proc~truncate_to_width->proc~escape_length Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code subroutine frame_set_line ( this , row , text ) !! Set one line, truncated to the frame width in code points. !! !! A row outside the frame is ignored rather than an error: a caller !! drawing a list longer than the terminal should not have to check !! every write. class ( frame_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this integer ( default_int ), intent ( in ) :: row !! One-based row. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: text !! Text for the row. if ( row < 1_default_int . or . row > this % rows ) return this % current ( row ) = truncate_to_width ( text , this % cols ) end subroutine frame_set_line","tags":"","url":"proc/frame_set_line.html"},{"title":"match_sequence – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine match_sequence(seq, complete, event, consumed) Test whether seq is a finished escape sequence. complete false means “could still become one, keep accumulating”. complete true with KEY_NONE means “definitely not one” and the\nbytes are discarded, which is how an unknown sequence resynchronises. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: seq Bytes accumulated so far, starting with ESC. logical, intent(out) :: complete Whether a decision has been reached. type( key_event_t ), intent(out) :: event The decoded key, or KEY_NONE if the sequence is unknown. integer(kind=default_int), intent(out) :: consumed How many leading bytes of seq the decision used. Called by proc~~match_sequence~~CalledByGraph proc~match_sequence match_sequence proc~decode_keys decode_keys proc~decode_keys->proc~match_sequence Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=default_int), private :: n Source Code pure subroutine match_sequence ( seq , complete , event , consumed ) !! Test whether `seq` is a finished escape sequence. !! !! `complete` false means \"could still become one, keep accumulating\". !! `complete` true with `KEY_NONE` means \"definitely not one\" and the !! bytes are discarded, which is how an unknown sequence resynchronises. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: seq !! Bytes accumulated so far, starting with ESC. logical , intent ( out ) :: complete !! Whether a decision has been reached. type ( key_event_t ), intent ( out ) :: event !! The decoded key, or `KEY_NONE` if the sequence is unknown. integer ( default_int ), intent ( out ) :: consumed !! How many leading bytes of `seq` the decision used. integer ( default_int ) :: n complete = . false . event % code = KEY_NONE event % char_code = 0_int32 consumed = len ( seq , kind = default_int ) n = len ( seq , kind = default_int ) if ( n == 1_default_int ) return ! just ESC so far ! `ESC [` and `ESC O` both introduce the sequences below; xterm emits ! the first in normal mode and the second in application cursor mode, ! and a program that handles only one loses the arrow keys in the other. if ( seq ( 2 : 2 ) /= \"[\" . and . seq ( 2 : 2 ) /= \"O\" ) then ! Nothing can continue from here, so the ESC was the Escape key. Only ! the ESC is consumed: the byte after it has not been classified yet ! and may itself start a sequence, as it does when the user presses ! Escape and then an arrow key inside one read. complete = . true . event % code = KEY_ESC consumed = 1_default_int return end if if ( n == 2_default_int ) return select case ( seq ( 3 : 3 )) case ( \"A\" ) event % code = KEY_UP case ( \"B\" ) event % code = KEY_DOWN case ( \"C\" ) event % code = KEY_RIGHT case ( \"D\" ) event % code = KEY_LEFT case ( \"H\" ) event % code = KEY_HOME case ( \"F\" ) event % code = KEY_END case ( \"1\" , \"3\" , \"4\" , \"7\" , \"8\" ) ! numeric forms: ESC [ 3 ~ and friends, which need the tilde if ( n == 3_default_int ) return if ( seq ( 4 : 4 ) /= \"~\" ) then complete = . true . return end if select case ( seq ( 3 : 3 )) case ( \"1\" , \"7\" ) event % code = KEY_HOME case ( \"4\" , \"8\" ) event % code = KEY_END case ( \"3\" ) event % code = KEY_DELETE case default ! Unreachable: the outer select already narrowed seq(3:3) to these ! five. Spelled out so the reader, and the linter, do not have to ! hold the outer case list in mind to see that it is total. event % code = KEY_NONE end select complete = . true . consumed = 4_default_int return case default complete = . true . ! unknown; drop it return end select complete = . true . consumed = 3_default_int end subroutine match_sequence","tags":"","url":"proc/match_sequence.html"},{"title":"pending_clear – PIC","text":"private pure subroutine pending_clear(this) Forget any partial escape sequence. Type Bound pending_t Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( pending_t ), intent(inout) :: this Called by proc~~pending_clear~~CalledByGraph proc~pending_clear pending_t%pending_clear proc~decode_keys decode_keys proc~decode_keys->proc~pending_clear Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Source Code pure subroutine pending_clear ( this ) !! Forget any partial escape sequence. class ( pending_t ), intent ( inout ) :: this this % buffer = \"\" this % length = 0_default_int end subroutine pending_clear","tags":"","url":"proc/pending_clear.html"},{"title":"dp_radix_sort – PIC","text":"module subroutine dp_radix_sort(array, work, reverse, err) Uses iso_c_binding proc~~dp_radix_sort~~UsesGraph proc~dp_radix_sort dp_radix_sort iso_c_binding iso_c_binding proc~dp_radix_sort->iso_c_binding Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: