d, err) Read a real(sp) array record. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit real(kind=sp), intent(out), allocatable :: values (:) logical, intent(in) :: swapped type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err private  subroutine read_record_header (unit, expected_tag, swapped, count, ok, err) Read a record header, check the type tag, and bounds check the\nannounced payload against the real length of the file. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: expected_tag logical, intent(in) :: swapped integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: count logical, intent(out) :: ok type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err private  subroutine write_char_record (unit, text, err) Write a character scalar record. The stored count is the number of\ncharacters, so a record of n characters occupies n payload bytes. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit character(len=*), intent(in) :: text type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err private  subroutine write_int32_array (unit, values, err) Write an integer(int32) array record. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: values (:) type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err private  subroutine write_int64_array (unit, values, err) Write an integer(int64) array record. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: values (:) type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err private  subroutine write_real_dp_array (unit, values, err) Write a real(dp) array record. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit real(kind=dp), intent(in) :: values (:) type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err private  subroutine write_real_sp_array (unit, values, err) Write a real(sp) array record. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit real(kind=sp), intent(in) :: values (:) type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err private  subroutine write_record_header (unit, tag, count, ok, err) Write a record header: type tag followed by the element count. ok rather than err carries the outcome so that a stale error left\nin err by an earlier call cannot be mistaken for a failure here. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: unit integer(kind=int32), intent(in) :: tag integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: count logical, intent(out) :: ok type( error_t ), intent(inout) :: err","tags":"","url":"module/pic_serialize.html"},{"title":"pic_random_dist – PIC","text":"Distributions whose output is an integer or a logical, and which are\ntherefore bit-identical on every supported compiler. Why a separate module from the real-valued one A textbook exponential deviate is -mean * log(u) . That calls libm,\nand libm is not the same function on every platform: GNU, Intel and\nNVIDIA do not agree to the last bit, and they are not obliged to. Any\nvalue derived that way is unusable in state that has to be compared\nacross runs or across compilers. Everything in this module is computed with integer arithmetic only –\nno real type appears in any expression that reaches a result – so a\ngiven generator and seed produce the same sequence everywhere. The\ntests pin the first outputs of every routine for a fixed seed, and\nthose pins are the contract. The real-valued distributions live in pic_random_dist_real . Keeping\nthem in a different module means a translation unit that only uses this\none cannot reach a non-reproducible deviate by accident. Quick start use pic_rng , only : splitmix64_t use pic_random_dist , only : next_range , next_bernoulli_ppm , & next_exponential_int type ( splitmix64_t ) :: gen call gen % seed ( 20250914_int64 ) die = next_range ( gen , 1 , 6 ) ! inclusive stolen = next_bernoulli_ppm ( gen , 2500 ) ! 0.25% chance gap_ms = next_exponential_int ( gen , 4000_int64 ) ! mean 4 seconds Uses pic_rng pic_uint64 pic_types pic_error module~~pic_random_dist~~UsesGraph module~pic_random_dist pic_random_dist module~pic_error pic_error module~pic_random_dist->module~pic_error module~pic_rng pic_rng module~pic_random_dist->module~pic_rng module~pic_types pic_types module~pic_random_dist->module~pic_types module~pic_uint64 pic_uint64 module~pic_random_dist->module~pic_uint64 module~pic_error->module~pic_types module~pic_global_definitions pic_global_definitions module~pic_error->module~pic_global_definitions module~pic_rng->module~pic_error module~pic_rng->module~pic_types module~pic_rng->module~pic_uint64 iso_c_binding iso_c_binding module~pic_types->iso_c_binding iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~pic_types->iso_fortran_env module~pic_uint64->module~pic_types module~pic_global_definitions->module~pic_types Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer(kind=int64), public, parameter :: EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN = 2147483647_int64 Largest mean next_exponential_int accepts, huge(int32) as an int64 . The bound is what makes the final fixed-point multiply\nprovably free of signed overflow; see scale_by_mean . integer(kind=default_int), public, parameter :: POISSON_MAX_MEAN_MILLI = 1000000_default_int Largest mean_milli next_poisson_int accepts, that is a mean of\n1000 events. The sampler is O(mean), so this is a guard against\nsilently getting slow rather than an arithmetic limit. integer(kind=default_int), public, parameter :: PPM_ONE = 1000000_default_int Parts per million corresponding to certainty, for next_bernoulli_ppm . integer(kind=default_int), private, parameter :: EXP_CELLS = 4096_default_int Number of cells in EXP_TABLE , and so 2**EXP_CELL_BITS. integer(kind=default_int), private, parameter :: EXP_CELL_BITS = 12_default_int Number of random bits that select a table cell; 2**12 == 4096. integer(kind=int64), private, parameter :: EXP_LN_CELLS = EXP_TABLE(EXP_TOP_CELL) ln(4096) in Q32.32. Taken from the table rather than written out\nagain, because -ln(1 - 4095/4096) is exactly ln(4096) and a second\ncopy of it could drift. Added once per turn around the tail loop. integer(kind=default_int), private, parameter :: EXP_MAX_TAIL = 8_default_int Most times the tail loop may go round. Each turn needs the 12-bit\ndraw to hit exactly one value, so reaching the cap has probability\n4096* -8, about 3e-29, and bounds the result at 9 ln(4096) means. integer(kind=int64), private, parameter :: EXP_TABLE (0:EXP_TOP_CELL) = [0_int64, 1048704_int64, 2097664_int64, 3146881_int64, 4196353_int64, 5246083_int64, 6296069_int64, 7346311_int64, 8396811_int64, 9447567_int64, 10498581_int64, 11549852_int64, 12601380_int64, 13653166_int64, 14705209_int64, 15757511_int64, 16810070_int64, 17862887_int64, 18915962_int64, 19969295_int64, 21022887_int64, 22076738_int64, 23130847_int64, 24185215_int64, 25239841_int64, 26294727_int64, 27349872_int64, 28405276_int64, 29460940_int64, 30516863_int64, 31573046_int64, 32629488_int64, 33686191_int64, 34743153_int64, 35800376_int64, 36857859_int64, 37915602_int64, 38973606_int64, 40031871_int64, 41090397_int64, 42149183_int64, 43208231_int64, 44267539_int64, 45327110_int64, 46386941_int64, 47447034_int64, 48507389_int64, 49568006_int64, 50628884_int64, 51690025_int64, 52751428_int64, 53813094_int64, 54875022_int64, 55937212_int64, 56999665_int64, 58062381_int64, 59125361_int64, 60188603