splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw unless ppm is 0 or PPM_ONE . integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: ppm Probability in parts per million, clamped to [0, PPM_ONE] . Return Value logical private  function pcg32_next_bernoulli_ppm (gen, ppm) result(r) True with probability ppm in a million, from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw unless ppm is 0 or PPM_ONE . integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: ppm Probability in parts per million, clamped to [0, PPM_ONE] . Return Value logical public        interface next_exponential_int Draw an exponentially distributed integer with the given mean, in\nwhatever unit mean is expressed in. Sampling is a table lookup and two integer multiplies. EXP_TABLE holds the inverse CDF -ln(1 - k/4096) in Q32.32 fixed point,\ngenerated once by tools/autogen/gen_exp_table.py with 60-digit\ndecimal arithmetic. Twelve random bits choose a cell, thirty-one more\ninterpolate linearly inside it, and the result is scaled by mean . The tail The top cell runs to infinity and cannot be interpolated into. It is\nhandled with the memorylessness of the exponential instead: if U is\nuniform on the top cell then -ln(1 - U) is distributed as ln(4096) plus a fresh exponential, so the sampler adds ln(4096) and draws again. That is exact, and it costs an extra draw with\nprobability 1/4096. Capping the top entry at a finite value and interpolating into it –\nthe obvious alternative – biases the mean by 0.147%. This scheme\nleaves 0.002%, and all of that residue is linear interpolation inside\nthe body cells, not the tail. mean must be in [0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN] . A mean of zero always\nreturns zero without consuming a draw. Out of range returns zero and,\nif err is present, sets ERROR_VALIDATION . private  function splitmix64_next_exponential_int (gen, mean, err) result(r) Exponential integer with the given mean, from a splitmix64_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by one draw, or more in the tail. integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: mean Mean of the distribution, in [0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int64) private  function pcg32_next_exponential_int (gen, mean, err) result(r) Exponential integer with the given mean, from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by one draw, or more in the tail. integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: mean Mean of the distribution, in [0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int64) public        interface next_poisson_int Draw a Poisson-distributed count with mean mean_milli/1000 . The mean is given a thousand times over so that fractional means are\nexpressible without a real type: mean_milli = 2500 means 2.5 events. Counts the exponential inter-arrivals that fit inside a fixed window,\nwhich is the defining construction of a Poisson process rather than\nan approximation of one. Cost is O(mean). mean_milli must be in [0, POISSON_MAX_MEAN_MILLI] . Zero returns\nzero without consuming a draw. Out of range returns zero and, if err is present, sets ERROR_VALIDATION . private  function splitmix64_next_poisson_int (gen, mean_milli, err) result(r) Poisson count with mean mean_milli/1000 , from a splitmix64_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced once per inter-arrival drawn. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: mean_milli A thousand times the mean, in [0, POISSON_MAX_MEAN_MILLI] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean_milli is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int32) private  function pcg32_next_poisson_int (gen, mean_milli, err) result(r) Poisson count with mean mean_milli/1000 , from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced once per inter-arrival drawn. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: mean_milli A thousand times the mean, in [0, POISSON_MAX_MEAN_MILLI] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean_milli is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int32) public        interface next_range Draw a uniform integer in the inclusive range [lo, hi] . The span is computed in unsigned 64-bit arithmetic, so the full range\nof default_int works: next_range(gen, -huge(1)-1, huge(1)) spans\nevery int32 value and is still exactly uniform. Rejection sampling\ngives every value in range exactly one preimage per draw, so there is\nno modulo bias. hi < lo is a usage error: it returns lo and, if err is present,\nsets ERROR_VALIDATION . lo == hi returns lo without consuming a\ndraw. private  function splitmix64_next_range (gen, lo, hi, err) result(r) Uniform integer in [lo, hi] from a splitmix64_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: lo Inclusive lower bound. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: hi Inclusive upper bound; must not be below lo . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when hi < lo . Return Value integer(kind=default_int) private  function pcg32_next_range (gen, lo, hi, err) result(r) Uniform integer in [lo, hi] from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: lo Inclusive lower bound. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: hi Inclusive upper bound; must not be below lo . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when hi < lo . Return Value integer(kind=default_int) Functions private  function pcg32_next_bernoulli_ppm (gen, ppm) result(r) True with probability ppm in a million, from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw unless ppm is 0 or PPM_ONE . integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: ppm Probability in parts per million, clamped to [0, PPM_ONE] . Return Value logical private  function pcg32_next_exponential_int (gen, mean, err) result(r) Exponential integer with the given mean, from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by one draw, or more in the tail. integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: mean Mean of the distribution, in [0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int64) private  function pcg32_next_poisson_int (gen, mean_milli, err) result(r) Poisson count with mean mean_milli/1000 , from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced once per inter-arrival drawn. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: mean_milli A thousand times the mean, in [0, POISSON_MAX_MEAN_MILLI] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean_milli is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int32) private  function pcg32_next_range (gen, lo, hi, err) result(r) Uniform integer in [lo, hi] from a pcg32_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: lo Inclusive lower bound. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: hi Inclusive upper bound; must not be below lo . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when hi < lo . Return Value integer(kind=default_int) private  function pcg32_uniform_below (gen, span_minus_1) result(r) Uniform unsigned integer in [0, span_minus_1] , by rejection. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( pcg32_t ), intent(inout) :: gen integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: span_minus_1 Inclusive upper bound, read as unsigned. Return Value integer(kind=int64) private pure function rejection_mask (span_minus_1) result(mask) Smallest 2**k - 1 that covers span_minus_1 read as unsigned. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: span_minus_1 One less than the number of distinct values wanted, unsigned. Return Value integer(kind=int64) private pure function scale_by_mean (x_q32, mean) result(r) Multiply a Q32.32 value by an integer mean and drop the fraction. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: x_q32 Deviate in Q32.32 fixed point. integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: mean Mean, in [0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN] . Return Value integer(kind=int64) private  function splitmix64_next_bernoulli_ppm (gen, ppm) result(r) True with probability ppm in a million, from a splitmix64_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw unless ppm is 0 or PPM_ONE . integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: ppm Probability in parts per million, clamped to [0, PPM_ONE] . Return Value logical private  function splitmix64_next_exponential_int (gen, mean, err) result(r) Exponential integer with the given mean, from a splitmix64_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by one draw, or more in the tail. integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: mean Mean of the distribution, in [0, EXPONENTIAL_MAX_MEAN] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int64) private  function splitmix64_next_poisson_int (gen, mean_milli, err) result(r) Poisson count with mean mean_milli/1000 , from a splitmix64_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced once per inter-arrival drawn. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: mean_milli A thousand times the mean, in [0, POISSON_MAX_MEAN_MILLI] . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when mean_milli is out of range. Return Value integer(kind=int32) private  function splitmix64_next_range (gen, lo, hi, err) result(r) Uniform integer in [lo, hi] from a splitmix64_t generator. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen Generator, advanced by the draw. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: lo Inclusive lower bound. integer(kind=default_int), intent(in) :: hi Inclusive upper bound; must not be below lo . type( error_t ), intent(inout), optional :: err Set to ERROR_VALIDATION when hi < lo . Return Value integer(kind=default_int) private  function splitmix64_uniform_below (gen, span_minus_1) result(r) Uniform unsigned integer in [0, span_minus_1] , by rejection. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( splitmix64_t ), intent(inout) :: gen integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: span_minus_1 Inclusive upper bound, read as unsigned. Return Value integer(kind=int64)","tags":"","url":"module/pic_random_dist.html"},{"title":"pic_tokenizer – PIC","text":"Splitting of character data into tokens and parsing of those tokens into\nnumbers with explicit error reporting through error_t . The parsing routines never fail silently: every malformed input produces\nan ERROR_PARSE error with a human readable message, and the output\nvalue is set to zero. They deliberately do not use list directed\ninternal reads, because those accept input that is almost never what the\ncaller meant ( \"1,2\" reads as 1, \"3*7\" is a repeat count, \"nan\" and \"infinity\" are accepted, and trailing junk after a separator is\nignored). Every accepted form is checked against an explicit grammar\nfirst. Empty field policy of split split keeps empty fields, so the number of returned elements is always\nthe number of delimiter occurrences plus one: split(\"a,,b\", \",\") returns three elements, \"a\" , \"\" and \"b\" split(\"\", \",\") returns one element, the empty string split(\",a\", \",\") returns \"\" and \"a\" , split(\"a,\", \",\") returns \"a\" and \"\" split(\"abc\", \",\") returns one element, \"abc\" This makes join(split(text, delim), delim) reproduce text exactly\n(for fields without trailing blanks) and keeps positional data such as\nCSV records aligned. Use tokenize for the other common policy, where\nruns of separators collapse and empty fields are dropped. A zero length delimiter cannot match anything, so split(text, \"\") returns the whole of text as a single element. Uses pic_ascii pic_error pic_string_type pic_types pic_strings module~~pic_tokenizer~~UsesGraph module~pic_tokenizer pic_tokenizer module~pic_ascii pic_ascii module~pic_tokenizer->module~pic_ascii module~pic_error pic_error module~pic_tokenizer->module~pic_error module~pic_string_type pic_string_type module~pic_tokenizer->module~pic_string_type module~pic_strings pic_strings module~pic_tokenizer->module~pic_strings module~pic_types pic_types module~pic_tokenizer->module~pic_types module~pic_ascii->module~pic_types module~pic_error->module~pic_types module~pic_global_definitions pic_global_definitions module~pic_error->module~pic_global_definitions module~pic_string_type->module~pic_ascii module~pic_string_type->module~pic_types module~pic_optional_value pic_optional_value module~pic_string_type->module~pic_optional_value module~pic_strings->module~pic_ascii module~pic_strings->module~pic_string_type module~pic_strings->module~pic_types iso_c_binding iso_c_binding module~pic_strings->iso_c_binding module~pic_strings->module~pic_optional_value module~pic_types->iso_c_binding iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~pic_types->iso_fortran_env module~pic_global_definitions->module~pic_types module~pic_optional_value->module~pic_types Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Used by module~~pic_tokenizer~~UsedByGraph module~pic_tokenizer pic_tokenizer module~pic_cli pic_cli module~pic_cli->module~pic_tokenizer Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Where possible, edges connecting nodes are\ngiven different colours to make them easier to distinguish in\nlarge graphs. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial character(len=*), private, parameter :: EXPONENT_MARKERS = \"eEdD\" Exponent letters accepted by parse_real ; q / Q is not accepted character(len=*), private, parameter :: SIGNS = \"+-\" Characters accepted as a leading sign of a number or of an exponent Interfaces public        interface parse_int Parse a string into an integer, reporting failures through error_t . The accepted grammar is optional surrounding whitespace, an optional + or - sign and one or more decimal digits, with nothing else. An empty\nor blank string, a missing digit, any stray character (including trailing\njunk such as \"12abc\" ) and a value too large for the requested kind all\nset ERROR_PARSE and leave value at zero. Specific procedures exist for int32 and int64 , so an argument\ndeclared integer(default_int) resolves correctly in both the default\nand the USE_INT8 build. private pure subroutine parse_int_int32_char (str, value, err) Parse a character string into a 32 bit integer. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: str integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: value Fixed width on purpose: the narrow specific procedure of the\ngeneric. type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err private pure subroutine parse_int_int32_string (str, value, err) Parse a string_type into a 32 bit integer. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( string_type ), intent(in) :: str integer(kind=int32), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err private pure subroutine parse_int_int64_char (str, value, err) Parse a character string into a 64 bit integer. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: str integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: value Fixed width on purpose: this is the widest integer the parser\naccumulates in, and the narrower specific procedure range checks\nagainst it. type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err private pure subroutine parse_int_int64_string (str, value, err) Parse a string_type into a 64 bit integer. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( string_type ), intent(in) :: str integer(kind=int64), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err public        interface parse_real Parse a string into a real, reporting failures through error_t . The accepted grammar is optional surrounding whitespace, an optional\nsign, a mantissa holding at least one digit and at most one decimal point\n(so both \".5\" and \"5.\" are accepted, \".\" is not), and an optional\nexponent introduced by e , E , d or D with an optional sign and at\nleast one digit. Everything else is rejected, in particular the spellings that a list\ndirected read would happily accept: \"nan\" , \"inf\" , \"infinity\" ,\nrepeat counts such as \"3*7\" , comma separated values such as \"1,2\" ,\nand any trailing junk. Values that overflow the requested kind are\nrejected as well instead of becoming infinity. Specific procedures exist for sp and dp . private  subroutine parse_real_sp_char (str, value, err) Parse a character string into a single precision real. The conversion is done in double precision and then range checked, so\na value that is finite in dp but too large for sp is reported as\nan error instead of becoming infinity. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: str real(kind=sp), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err private  subroutine parse_real_sp_string (str, value, err) Parse a string_type into a single precision real. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( string_type ), intent(in) :: str real(kind=sp), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err private  subroutine parse_real_dp_char (str, value, err) Parse a character string into a double precision real. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: str real(kind=dp), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err private  subroutine parse_real_dp_string (str, value, err) Parse a string_type into a double precision real. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( string_type ), intent(in) :: str real(kind=dp), intent(out) :: value type( error_t ), intent(out) :: err public        interface split Split a string on every occurrence of a delimiter, keeping empty fields. The result always holds one more element than there are delimiter\noccurrences, so an empty input yields one empty element. See the module\ndocumentation for the full policy. private pure function split_char_char (string, delimiter) result(parts) Split a character string on a character delimiter. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: string character(len=*), intent(in) :: delimiter Return Value type( string_type ), allocatable, (:) private pure function split_char_string (string, delimiter) result(parts) Split a character string on a string_type delimiter. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: string type( string_type ), intent(in) :: delimiter Return Value type( string_type ), allocatable, (:) private pure function split_string_char (string, delimiter) result(parts) Split a string_type on a character delimiter. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( string_type ), intent(in) :: string character(len=*), intent(in) :: delimiter Return Value type( string_type ), allocatable, (:) private pure function split_string_string (string, delimiter) result(parts) Split a string_type on a string_type delimiter. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( string_type ), intent(in) :: string type( string_type ), intent(in) :: delimiter Return Value type( string_type ), allocatable, (:) public        interface tokenize Split a string into whitespace separated words. Runs of whitespace are treated as a single separator and empty fields are\ndiscarded, so a string that is empty or entirely whitespace yields a zero\nsized result. Whitespace is the set defined by pic_ascii , that is space,\nhorizontal tab, vertical tab, carriage return, line feed and form feed. private pure function tokenize_char (string) result(tokens) Split a character string into whitespace separated words. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: string Return Value type( string_type ), allocatable, (:) private pure function tokenize_string (string) result(tokens) Split a string_type into whitespace separated words. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( string_type ), intent(in) :: string Return Value type( string_type ), allocatable, (:) Functions 